<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Long-press the Yosher icon and the app opens ALREADY LISTENING.

    ── NOT A LINE OF JAVA, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN ──────────────────────────────

    The shortcut fires `yosher://tell`. Capacitor hands that url to the page
    (`getLaunchUrl` on a cold start, `appUrlOpen` on a warm one) and
    `tell-launcher.tsx` decides what it means. The web decides what the app
    shows (ADR 0032), so the shell declares the door and nothing else — which
    is also why changing what a long-press DOES needs a web deploy and not a
    store release.

    ── WHY A CUSTOM SCHEME AND NOT AN https URL ────────────────────────────────

    A shortcut firing `https://yosherapp.com/dashboard?tell=1` opens the
    phone's BROWSER unless the app has verified App Links — which needs an
    `assetlinks.json` carrying the signing certificate's fingerprint, and the
    debug key and the Play key have different ones. It would work for exactly
    one of the two builds anybody is holding. A scheme the app owns has none of
    that.

    `android:enabled="true"` and a static shortcut rather than a dynamic one:
    there is nothing to compute, it is the same shortcut for everybody, and a
    static one is there the moment the app is installed rather than after the
    first launch.
-->
<shortcuts xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="tell"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_tell"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_tell_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_tell_long">
        <intent
            android:action="android.intent.action.VIEW"
            android:data="yosher://tell"
            android:targetPackage="com.yosherapp.app"
            android:targetClass="com.yosherapp.app.MainActivity" />
    </shortcut>
</shortcuts>
